projects.description:
As the Bougainville referendum target date approaches, ensuring that the population is adequately prepared to make an informed decision on the region’s political future is vital. This activity is critical for safety and security in the region, with the population looking to engage in dialogue and the BPA implementation, while at the same time increasing their participation in the civic space. 

To add to the awareness raising activities of the GYPI and BRSP projects, this activity will support interventions that go beyond just an awareness of the referendum and the BPA. Through this activity, storytelling, community dialogue, use of innovative technology, theatre and art will be used to tell the peacebuilding story, increase awareness and understanding of the BPA and the referendum, and discuss possible post-referendum issues. Lessons from the past indicate that while information and communication about the referendum and BPA is needed – what is also needed, and is just as important, is for communities to develop their own, joint vision about what a peaceful and prosperous community will look like in the future, and the role they can play in achieving that vision. This requires an inductive rather than a didactic approach to engaging communities. This will need to be led by a range of different community leaders, youth, women, churches, CSOs and local government to forge a shared vision. 

Key outputs from this activity are:

(i)	Both governments agree on joint messages on the BPA, including referendum, and facilitate their dissemination;

(ii) 	Innovative community-led dialogues about the BPA and a peaceful future for Bougainville are facilitated throughout the island;

(iii)      BPA dialogue and referendum awareness raising increases within Papua New Guinea.

Approval of the amendments to the Climate Change Management Act (2015), Forestry Act and design of National Sustainable Land Use Policy are the impetus for:
1) The development of a National REDD+ Finance and Investment Plan completed and endorsed by the Government;
2) PNG Palm Oil Platform officially launched and National Action Plan developed;
3) Summary of Information on Cancun Safeguards developed and submitted to the UNFCCC and Government for approval.

These three key activities will be undertaken in parallel to legal and policy work.

Specific activities under this key activity are listed below and are to be coordinated by different officers within the UNICEF WASH program, however WASH Chief is overall coordinator;
- Develop Community Water Safety Planning Guidelines  [Highlands Programming-One UN].
- Support establishment of Ceramic Water Filter production and develop capacity for local manufacturing.

Specific activities under this key activity are listed below and are to be coordinated by different officers within the UNICEF WASH program, however WASH Chief is overall coordinator;
- Third party monitoring and reporting on the quality of WASH interventions.
- Operational expenses to support field travel for assurance activities

